u-boot: an autoboot you can actually interrupt, at a rate you can type at

Two lines of configs/rk3399_defconfig, and between them they are the
difference between a board you can debug and a board you can only recover
with a USB cable and a button.

CONFIG_BOOTDELAY=0. The vendor defconfig boots straight through, so there is
no U-Boot prompt on this image at all - no way to pick a different boot
device, load a kernel over the network, or read back a partition when the
kernel is the thing that is broken. It was learned the hard way: an
experiment that left a non-booting bootloader on the eMMC had no software
route back, because the BootROM reads eMMC before it looks at the card (see
docs/emmc.md) and nothing in between ever stops to ask. Recovery meant
maskrom mode, a USB-C cable and the MASKROM key. Two seconds of autoboot
delay is the cheapest insurance in this tree.

CONFIG_BAUDRATE=1500000. 1500000 is the Rockchip convention and every RK3399
defconfig ships it, so this is the line to justify rather than the other one.
It comes down to the debug header having three pins.

Measured on this board, CH341 straight to the header, 1900 bytes each way:

    115200   board -> host  1900/1900     host -> board  1900/1900
    1500000  board -> host  1900/1900     host -> board  1878/1900

and /proc/tty/driver/serial shows why - two overruns, and zero framing or
parity errors. The bytes arrive intact and the receiver drops them. Send the
same 1900 bytes in 32-byte chunks 2 ms apart and it is 1900/1900 with the
overrun count unchanged, which settles it: the link is clean at 1500000 and
what fails is a sustained burst.

That is structural, not a fault. ttyS2 reports base_baud = 1500000, i.e. a
24 MHz uartclk and a divisor of exactly 1 - the ceiling for this UART - into
a 64-byte FIFO with no CTS, because the header carries TX, RX and ground and
nothing else. Anything longer than a keystroke, a pasted command in
particular, is a burst. 115200 has 13x the slack and needs no flow control to
survive one.

The other thing worth ruling out is the adapter. An RS-232 transceiver in the
path - the common USB-to-RS232 into an RS-232-to-TTL board rig - is specified
to 235 kbps in the SP3232E/MAX3232 family, so 1500000 is far outside its
sheet. That has not been measured here and at least one person reports that
rig working, so treat it as the first thing to swap out rather than as the
explanation.

This supersedes an earlier note in this tree that said the board "corrupts on
write" at 1500000. It does not. The bytes arrive intact; the receiver drops
them when they arrive faster than it drains them, and only then.

The kernel console was already at 115200 (bootargs, patch 0001 of the kernel
series), so this also stops the console changing speed halfway through every
boot. The trust.img blob still prints its own lines at 1500000; those few
bytes of garbage are the only thing left that does.

diff --git a/configs/rk3399_defconfig b/configs/rk3399_defconfig
index c83fd8aa..4296b014 100755
--- a/configs/rk3399_defconfig
+++ b/configs/rk3399_defconfig
@@ -13,7 +13,7 @@ CONFIG_DEFAULT_DEVICE_TREE="rk3399-evb"
 CONFIG_DEBUG_UART=y
 CONFIG_FIT=y
 CONFIG_SPL_LOAD_FIT=y
-CONFIG_BOOTDELAY=0
+CONFIG_BOOTDELAY=2
 CONFIG_SYS_CONSOLE_INFO_QUIET=y
 # CONFIG_DISPLAY_CPUINFO is not set
 CONFIG_ANDROID_BOOTLOADER=y
@@ -96,7 +96,7 @@ CONFIG_SDRAM_COMMON_CAP_DETECT=y
 CONFIG_SDRAM_COMMON_OSREG=y
 CONFIG_SDRAM_COMMON_MSCH_RK3399=y
 CONFIG_DM_RESET=y
-CONFIG_BAUDRATE=1500000
+CONFIG_BAUDRATE=115200
 CONFIG_DEBUG_UART_BASE=0xFF1A0000
 CONFIG_DEBUG_UART_CLOCK=24000000
 CONFIG_DEBUG_UART_SHIFT=2
